# Cloudflare Pages _headers
# Security headers migrated from vercel.json (2026-08-04, Vercel -> Cloudflare Pages) merged
# with the pre-existing cache rules (do NOT drop the cache rules — legacy-shadow incident).

# Security headers on every response (from vercel.json).
# Cache-Control on /* covers directory indexes (/os/, /gspc-verify/, …)
# that do not match /*.html. Without it, Pages default s-maxage=604800
# hid a thin-shell production clobber behind a cached fat /os/ (2026-08-22).
/*
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), interest-cohort=()
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains

# Fingerprinted build chunks. NOTE: deliberately NOT `immutable`.
# 2026-08-11 white-screen incident: a deploy-skew miss served index.html (200, text/html)
# for a missing /assets/*.js, and `immutable, max-age=1yr` froze that HTML under the JS URL
# in browsers for a year — no reload or hard-refresh could clear it. `no-cache` makes every
# asset revalidate (cheap 304 on hashed names) so a bad copy self-heals on the next load.
# NOTE (2026-08-11, third recurrence): a hard 404 for a missing chunk is NOT expressible
# on Cloudflare Pages. `_redirects` supports 3xx and 200 only — a 404 status is silently
# ignored (that syntax is Netlify's). A Pages Function could return 404, but Pages excludes
# static-asset paths from Functions, so it would only run if /assets/* were routed through
# the Worker — which would put these very cache headers at risk. `no-cache` IS the working
# protection: it was verified on 2026-08-11 to heal an already-poisoned browser entry
# (cached text/html under a .js URL) on the next load.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# HTML shell: always revalidate (no blanket s-maxage — legacy-shadow incident).
/*.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate

/
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate

# A2A v1.0 agent card (RFC 8615, N5-13)
/.well-known/agent-card.json
  Content-Type: application/a2a+json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

# Evidence tree. /signed/HOW-TO-VERIFY.md was served as text/markdown, which every
# browser DOWNLOADS rather than renders — so the one document we point IETF
# implementers at arrived as a file in their Downloads folder instead of a page.
# text/plain renders inline and is still perfectly machine-readable. The bytes are
# untouched; only the Content-Type changes.
/signed/HOW-TO-VERIFY.md
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate

# The generated human indexes for the evidence tree (scripts/generate-signed-index.mjs).
# Must revalidate: a stale index that lists artifacts the tree no longer serves is worse
# than no index, because it reads as evidence.
/signed/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate

/signed/cards/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate

# Interop evidence must be READABLE in a browser, not downloaded.
# Cloudflare Pages serves .py as application/octet-stream, and the global
# `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` above means a browser will not second-guess it: the tab
# renders blank while the bytes arrive intact. Reported 2026-09-03 after a reviewer opened
# /interop/scrapi-ccf/mint_data_hash_vector.py and saw an apparently empty file — 7818 bytes
# had in fact been served. These files are cited in IETF Last Call mail so that reviewers can
# read the minter and recompute the digests themselves; one that downloads instead of
# rendering reads as an empty publication, which is worse than no citation at all.
# Both patterns are listed because the splat form is what matches nested directories.
/interop/*.py
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

/interop/scrapi-ccf/mint_data_hash_vector.py
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
